BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Research Seminar in Microeconomics - Chris Höhne 
DTSTART:20251008T094500Z
DTEND:20251008T110000Z
DTSTAMP:20251202T212448Z
UID:53fvgv0lt2h5cf68k900o90ble@google.com
SEQUENCE:0
CREATED:20250922T073816Z
DESCRIPTION:<b><u>Speaker:</u></b> Chris Höhne<br><br><b><u>Title</u></b
 >: "Double-Edged Ballot: Affective Polarization Around the 2024 US Preside
 ntial Election"<br><br><b><u>Abstract:</u></b><br>Public concern about ris
 ing polarization in the United States conflicts with evidence suggesting t
 hat it has plateaued in recent years. We show that this paradox arises bec
 ause polarization in behavior diverges from stated polarization by conduct
 ing a three-wave online experiment around the 2024 presidential election. 
 We use incentivized trust and cooperation games to capture behavioral pola
 rization\, and compare it with stated polarization from survey metrics inc
 luding the standard thermometer gap. Behavioral polarization increases sha
 rply around the election\, while the standard thermometer gap remains unch
 anged. The effect is exclusively driven by an increase in swing states. De
 mocrats show higher hostility than Republicans across all metrics\, yet ch
 anges in behavior are uncorrelated with stated animosity\, underscoring th
 e divergence between metrics. <br>Our findings show that the thermometer 
 metric misses critical trends evident in actual behavior – polarization 
 is intensifying rather than plateauing in the U.S.
LAST-MODIFIED:20251006T124626Z
LOCATION:OMDS-Seminarroom\, 3rd floor
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
